# Upstream: giac 2.1.0 usual.cc numeric_matrix_exp, reached from exp of a square matrix whose entries are all floats (or pi; is_fully_numeric): the series ran while linfnorm(term)._DOUBLE_val>epsilon, and above Digits 14 the norm is an MPFR real, whose _DOUBLE_val reads other bits of the union as a tiny double, so the loop never ran and the identity came back: exp(evalf([[1,2],[3,4]])) and exp(evalf([[0,1],[-1,0]])) at Digits 15 and 40 = [[1,0],[0,1]] (right [[51.9689561987,74.736564567],[112.104846851,164.073803049]] and [[cos 1,sin 1],[-sin 1,cos 1]], mpmath expm); exp([[pi,pi],[pi,pi]]) the identity at any Digits. At Digits 12 the series was wrong too: the 100 terms of max_numexp did not reach a larger norm and the terms cancelled (exp(evalf([[0,40],[-40,0]])) gave -155.95 for cos 40 = -0.666938061652, exp(evalf([[-30,0],[0,-30]])) 0.00243 for exp(-30) = 9.35762296884e-14), and the last digit of [[1,2],[3,4]] was off (112.10484685); the factor gen(1/j) was a double and epsilon absolute. Two more faults met on the way: Giac's matrix product gives the zero matrix when every entry of a factor is within epsilon (1e-12) of 0 (evalf([[10^-19,0],[0,10^-19]])*evalf([[1,0],[0,1]]) = [[0,0],[0,0]] at Digits 40), and a float matrix times inv(gen(j)) in C++ (operator*, no context) took 1/j as a double. Now scaling and squaring: the matrix is divided by 2^s, s the least with n*max|entry|/2^s <= 1/2, the series runs at the entries' precision plus s+16 bits (53 for doubles), each product entry by entry and each scalar a float of those bits, until a bound of the rest is below the last bit; the sum is squared s times and rounded back to the entries' precision (doubles stay doubles, a complex entry too). An entry with no finite double is refused; a zero matrix still gives the exact identity; exact and symbolic matrices keep analytic_apply.
